WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.490
It takes a lot more than accurately re-creating
a comic book character's outfit on the big

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:07.420
screen to make a superhero movie worth seeing.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.420
And anyone who's seen the terrible film version
of Captain America from 1990 knows it.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.300
In terms of comic book costume accuracy, it's
a ten out of ten!

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.700
For having literally everything else be absolutely
terrible, it's a negative twelve-and-a-half.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:33.030
Needless to say, when former Marvel Studios
head of visual development Charlie Wen sat

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.989
down to adapt the classic Marvel Comics cosmic
super team the Guardians of the Galaxy, he

00:00:37.989 --> 00:00:40.580
had a lot more on his mind than comic book
accuracy.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:44.060
To get the right look for the right character,
Wen and his team tried beginning with the

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.820
right words before anything else, which informed
the design process.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:53.140
"Oftentimes if I'm designing, or if I'm working
with other designers that are in my department

00:00:53.140 --> 00:01:00.660
at the time, it's starting with those descriptors,
and more in that, what do those descriptors

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:01.460
remind you of?

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:08.040
And so there's just a lot of adjectives, actions,
verbs, anything that define who those characters

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.960
are, that I wanna make sure that we always
go back to that."

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.860
So what process did Wen take with these relatively
obscure Marvel D-listers to bring them into

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:16.870
the mainstream?

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:19.640
Here's what the Guardians of the Galaxy almost
looked like.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.380
Because the Guardians of the Galaxy were relatively
unknown characters before the movie broke

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.860
out big in 2014, Wen had a lot of freedom
to explore different designs for the film's

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.540
characters and settings.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:34.800
Part of that freedom, said Wen, came from
Marvel Studios chief Kevin Feige :

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.080
"Kevin is a true Star Wars fan."

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.840
“I think it was something like 20 something
times or in the theater, maybe 40.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:45.340
I don't remember, but he loves Star Wars.”

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:49.820
That, and the fact that the Guardians themselves
were pretty obscure in the public consciousness,

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:53.220
meant that Wen was free to create Marvel's
very own Star Wars.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.040
"This one also had a special place in my heart..."

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.400
"If you go with Iron Man, Thor, Captain America,
Hulk.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.780
All these characters are pretty defined.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:08.420
People know what they are and Guardians, nobody
knew what Guardians was.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:09.060
It's very niche.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:14.120
What was great was it also left it so that
we could do a lot with it.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:20.900
If you upset some of the hardcore fans, maybe
it's okay because there's not as many of them.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:28.500
But what it did do is it allowed for a lot
of exploration to create a Marvel Space Odyssey."

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:34.420
In 1976, writer Bill Mantlo wrote a spacefaring
comic book story called "The Sword in the

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:39.959
Star," introducing a new character drawn by
Keith Giffen: Rocky Raccoon - like the Beatles

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.520
song - not Rocket Raccoon - because they didn’t
think they’d get sued for that?

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:44.520
Anyway...

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:46.460
. Armed with a gun, a cigar, and a bunch of
attitude, Rocky came back six years later

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:50.420
with a new design as Rocket Raccoon when Mantlo
was writing The Incredible Hulk.

00:02:50.689 --> 00:02:54.640
The character would come back again and again
over the years, never really straying from

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:57.600
the core characteristics Mantlo had given
him at the start.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.200
These attributes played a huge role in Wen's
design work for Rocket.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.220
He explained the important details he wanted
to capture when designing Rocket:

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:11.340
"He's impatient, he's got the little guy syndrome,
the Napoleon syndrome.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:17.379
Definitely a big man in a little body, big
character in a little body, bursting out at

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:18.060
the seams.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:20.080
He's always up to eleven."

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:24.720
When you look at the concept art Wen created
during Guardians of the Galaxy's pre-production,

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.540
Rocket's huge personality comes through clearly.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:32.240
Even the way he's standing — battle-ready,
with a snarl on his face is perfectly contrasted

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.280
with Groot's presence in the background.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.340
And while we're on the subject of Groot…

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:40.520
Unlike Rocket, Groot evolved significantly
from his comics debut.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:46.160
In 1960, on the cover of Tales to Astonish
#13, a giant tree monster named "Groot from

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.840
Planet X" shows up on a busy city street on
Earth, daring someone to fight him.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:55.430
He returned as a monster 11 years later to
fight the Hulk, and finally started resembling

00:03:55.430 --> 00:04:00.930
our peaceful, heroic Groot when he appeared
in 2006's Nick Fury's Howling Commandos and

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:03.240
2007's Annihilation Conquest.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:07.439
In the latter series, he seemed to develop
the friendship with Rocket Raccoon that became

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.680
so central to the 2014 film.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:14.060
In fact, according to Wen, Groot and Rocket
were two of the biggest question marks going

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:15.840
into the film's pre-production period.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:22.580
"Nobody knew if this was gonna work, and everybody
was very nervous about Groot and Rocket, because

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:28.680
the whole movie was contingent upon those
two characters, a tree and a raccoon, especially

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.470
being an integral part of that five character
team."

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.600
Meanwhile, Wen contrasted this moss-topped
Groot with Rocket's fiery personality.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.420
When asked about the words he'd use to describe
the ideas he was trying to get across with

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.410
his early designs, Wen said:

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:51.120
"The background tree, when he needs to be,
but he's a nice, quiet, empathetic, sympathetic

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.500
addition to that group.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:57.320
The quiet stream that keeps a peace between
all of them."

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:01.580
Star-Lord has had plenty of looks over the
years, though a lot of them have kept one

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:02.100
constant.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:06.860
From his first appearance way back in 1976
and up through his more modern adventures

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:11.670
in Marvel's Guardians of the Galaxy team in
2007, Star-Lord seems to like wearing some

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:12.980
pretty weird headgear.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.840
Wen tried to capture this element in his concept
art.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.710
But the design presented some headaches.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:21.660
"A very German World War 2 military, the shape
of the helmet.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.100
Even the way the pants are.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:28.930
A lot of those, we certainly can't really
go for something that ... Even the color,

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:35.360
the blues, we can't go for something that's
gonna hearken back to World War II Nazi uniforms.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:42.020
It works with Hydra, for Captain America,
Hydra, Red Skull, that makes sense, and they're

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:42.520
villains."

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:47.220
The design work took a positive turn once
writer-director James Gunn came on board.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:52.370
Once James came in, the retro kick-back with
the music, with all that, was very specific

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:52.860
to James.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:58.240
He knew really the tone and feel he wanted
them to feel like, and so that was really

00:05:58.240 --> 00:05:58.740
important.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:04.080
Oftentimes, we don't always get that, and
so James was much more specific with saying,

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.310
'Peter Quill's gonna have a tape deck that's
important to him.'

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.600
So I'm like, 'Great, that makes our job so
much easier already.'

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:12.720
Because that is a visual cue.”

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:16.680
That's what led to the cinematic look — the
facemask and exposed hair.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:20.250
But the design communicated more about the
character than you might have realized.

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:25.050
"Having his hair be exposed, in a way it's
... Some people were thinking, 'How much does

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:26.840
that actually do to the character?

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:28.280
You're only showing the hair.'

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:30.640
I'm like, 'Well, but hair actually shows character.'

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.000
If it's unkempt and all over, you already
see a part of that character."

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:39.240
Nova was another important character from
Marvel Comics' batch of cosmic stories in

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:44.840
the 1970s, though the version we got in Guardians
of the Galaxy didn't quite resemble the Earth-based

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:47.570
superhero Rich Ryder who went by that name.

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:52.810
We didn't have the super hero Nova, as opposed
to the Nova Corps."

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:57.000
But just because Nova as a lone superhero
wasn't written into the script at the time

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.620
didn't mean he wouldn't make an appearance
in some fashion.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.440
As part of his explorations of Marvel's characters
and designs, Wen created some Nova concept

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:08.900
art that was inspired by Ryder's superheroic
alter ego to make sure the filmmakers had

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:10.580
as many options as possible.

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:14.760
"I knew at the time, I was like, 'Okay, well,
we're probably not gonna be having Nova as

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:15.770
the lone character.'

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:16.861
At least, it wasn't written in.

00:07:16.861 --> 00:07:19.750
I was playing on that line, just in case.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:24.150
So I did some takes where it's more heroic,
it stands out more, right?

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:26.980
As, 'I'm a significant character that needs
to stand out.'"

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:31.890
But while Wen's superheroic version Nova didn't
make it into the film, it's still pretty exciting

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:34.600
to know that his loss was John C. Reilly's
gain.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:35.220
"Hey!

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:37.300
If it isn't Star-Prince!"

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:39.100
"It's Star-Lord."

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:40.800
"Oh, sorry, Lord."

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:45.980
We did tons of takes, and I ended up doing
this one that's more like the military space

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:48.020
police, because that's what he serves.

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:51.160
So really, ultimately, it just needs to serve
the story."

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:52.000
“He's got a codename!”

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:55.860
“It’s an outlaw name.”

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:56.700
“Relax pal.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:59.190
It’s cool to have a codename.

00:07:59.190 --> 00:07:59.680
It’s not weird."

